## NEXT.JS APP ROUTER COMPREHENSIVE DOCUMENTATION SUMMARY

Based on my thorough reading of the official Next.js App Router documentation (15+ files from `/vercel/sandbox/node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/`), here's everything you need to understand about the App Router:

---

### 1. LAYOUTS (layout.tsx)

**What They Are:**
- Layouts are UI that is **shared** between multiple pages
- They preserve state, remain interactive, and do **not** re-render on navigation
- File: `layout.tsx` or `layout.js` in any folder within the `app` directory
- Required: Root layout at `app/layout.tsx` with `<html>` and `<body>` tags

**Key Features:**
- Nested automatically - layouts wrap child layouts via `children` prop
- Accept `children` prop which can be a page or another layout
- Can pass `params` prop (for dynamic segments)
- Server Components by default
- Root layout is **required** for every app

**Example:**
```typescript
// app/layout.tsx
export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        <nav>{/* Navigation */}</nav>
        <main>{children}</main>
      </body>
    </html>
  )
}

// app/blog/layout.tsx (nested layout)
export default function BlogLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return <section>{children}</section>
}
```

**Component Hierarchy:**
- Layout wraps everything in a folder
- Multiple levels nest: `app/layout.tsx` > `app/blog/layout.tsx` > `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx`

---

### 2. PAGES (page.tsx)

**What They Are:**
- A page is UI rendered on a **specific route**
- File: `page.tsx` or `page.js` in any folder
- Default export must be a React component
- Only the content from `page.js` and `route.js` is sent to the client

**Key Features:**
- Routes only become **public** when a `page.js` or `route.js` file exists
- Can be async (Server Component by default)
- Receive `params` and `searchParams` as props
- Rendered within parent layouts automatically

**Example:**
```typescript
// app/page.tsx (home page)
export default function Page() {
  return <h1>Hello, Next.js!</h1>
}

// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx (dynamic page)
export default async function BlogPostPage({
  params,
  searchParams,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
  searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const post = await getPost(slug)

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p>{post.content}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```

---

### 3. ROUTE HANDLERS (API Routes in App Router)

**What They Are:**
- Custom request handlers for a given route using Web Request/Response APIs
- File: `route.ts` or `route.js` (named routes)
- Replaces Pages Router's API Routes
- Only available in the `app` directory

**Key Features:**
- Support HTTP methods: `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`, `HEAD`, `OPTIONS`
- Returns `405 Method Not Allowed` for unsupported methods
- Can nest anywhere in `app` directory like `page.js`
- **Cannot** have both `route.js` and `page.js` at same level
- Use `NextRequest` and `NextResponse` APIs
- Not cached by default (except `GET` with `force-static`)
- Can use Cache Components with `use cache`

**Example:**
```typescript
// app/api/route.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server'

export async function GET(request: NextRequest) {
  const res = await fetch('https://data.mongodb-api.com/...', {
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      'API-Key': process.env.DATA_API_KEY,
    },
  })
  const data = await res.json()
  return NextResponse.json({ data })
}

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const body = await request.json()
  // Process the body
  return NextResponse.json({ success: true }, { status: 201 })
}

// Dynamic routes
// app/users/[id]/route.ts
export async function GET(
  request: Request,
  { params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }
) {
  const { id } = await params
  return NextResponse.json({ id })
}

// Force static caching
export const dynamic = 'force-static'
export async function GET() {
  const data = await fetch('...', { next: { revalidate: 3600 } })
  return NextResponse.json(data)
}
```

**Caching Behavior:**
- `GET` requests can be cached with `export const dynamic = 'force-static'`
- Other HTTP methods are **never** cached
- With Cache Components enabled, `GET` follows same model as UI routes

---

### 4. METADATA (Replacing Head Component)

**What It Does:**
- Adds metadata to pages for improved SEO and web shareability
- Replaces manual `<Head>` component approach
- Next.js automatically generates relevant `<head>` tags

**Three Ways to Define Metadata:**

**A. Static Metadata (Metadata object):**
```typescript
// app/blog/layout.tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'My Blog',
  description: 'Latest tech articles',
  keywords: ['blog', 'tech'],
  openGraph: {
    title: 'My Blog',
    description: 'Latest tech articles',
    url: 'https://example.com/blog',
    images: [{
      url: 'https://example.com/og-image.jpg',
      width: 1200,
      height: 630,
    }],
  },
}

export default function Layout() {}
```

**B. Dynamic Metadata (generateMetadata function):**
```typescript
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
import type { Metadata, ResolvingMetadata } from 'next'

type Props = {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
  searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>
}

export async function generateMetadata(
  { params, searchParams }: Props,
  parent: ResolvingMetadata
): Promise<Metadata> {
  const slug = (await params).slug
  const post = await fetch(`https://api.vercel.app/blog/${slug}`).then((res) =>
    res.json()
  )

  return {
    title: post.title,
    description: post.description,
    openGraph: {
      title: post.title,
      images: [post.imageUrl],
    },
  }
}

export default function Page() {}
```

**C. File-based Metadata:**
- `favicon.ico`, `apple-icon.jpg`, `icon.jpg` - App icons
- `opengraph-image.jpg`, `twitter-image.jpg` - Social media images
- `robots.txt` - Search engine robots
- `sitemap.xml` - Site map

**D. Generated OG Images (Dynamic):**
```typescript
// app/blog/[slug]/opengraph-image.tsx
import { ImageResponse } from 'next/og'

export const size = {
  width: 1200,
  height: 630,
}

export const contentType = 'image/png'

export default async function Image({ params }: { params: { slug: string } }) {
  const post = await getPost(params.slug)

  return new ImageResponse(
    (
      <div
        style={{
          fontSize: 128,
          background: 'white',
          width: '100%',
          height: '100%',
          display: 'flex',
          alignItems: 'center',
          justifyContent: 'center',
        }}
      >
        {post.title}
      </div>
    )
  )
}
```

**Key Features:**
- Exports only in Server Components
- `generateMetadata` can fetch data
- Streaming metadata supported for dynamic routes
- Default fields always added: charset and viewport meta tags

---

### 5. ERROR HANDLING (error.tsx, not-found.tsx)

**Two Categories of Errors:**

**A. Expected Errors (Handled Explicitly):**
- Form validation errors
- Failed requests
- Return errors as values, don't throw

```typescript
// app/actions.ts (Server Action)
'use server'

export async function createPost(prevState: any, formData: FormData) {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/posts', {
    method: 'POST',
    body: JSON.stringify(formData),
  })

  if (!res.ok) {
    return { message: 'Failed to create post' }
  }

  return { success: true }
}
```

```typescript
// app/ui/form.tsx (Client Component)
'use client'

import { useActionState } from 'react'
import { createPost } from '@/app/actions'

export function Form() {
  const [state, formAction, pending] = useActionState(createPost, { message: '' })

  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <input type="text" name="title" />
      {state?.message && <p>{state.message}</p>}
      <button disabled={pending}>Create Post</button>
    </form>
  )
}
```

**B. Uncaught Exceptions (Error Boundaries):**
- Use `error.tsx` files (Client Components)
- Bubble up to nearest parent error boundary
- Create fallback UI instead of crashing app

```typescript
// app/dashboard/error.tsx
'use client' // Must be Client Component

import { useEffect } from 'react'

export default function ErrorPage({
  error,
  reset,
}: {
  error: Error & { digest?: string }
  reset: () => void
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.error(error)
  }, [error])

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>
      <button onClick={() => reset()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

**Global Error Handling:**
```typescript
// app/global-error.tsx (for root layout errors)
'use client'

export default function GlobalError({
  error,
  reset,
}: {
  error: Error & { digest?: string }
  reset: () => void
}) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>
        <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>
        <button onClick={() => reset()}>Try again</button>
      </body>
    </html>
  )
}
```

**Not Found Errors:**
```typescript
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation'

export default async function Page({ params }: { params: { slug: string } }) {
  const post = getPostBySlug(params.slug)

  if (!post) {
    notFound() // Shows not-found.tsx
  }

  return <div>{post.title}</div>
}

// app/blog/[slug]/not-found.tsx
export default function NotFound() {
  return <div>404 - Post Not Found</div>
}
```

**Key Differences:**
- Error boundaries catch errors during rendering
- Event handler errors must be caught manually with try/catch
- Errors in `startTransition` bubble to nearest error boundary

---

### 6. DATA FETCHING (Replacing getServerSideProps, getStaticProps, getStaticPaths)

**In Server Components:**
```typescript
// app/blog/page.tsx

// With fetch API
export default async function Page() {
  const data = await fetch('https://api.vercel.app/blog')
  const posts = await data.json()

  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}

// With database/ORM
import { db, posts } from '@/lib/db'

export default async function Page() {
  const allPosts = await db.select().from(posts)

  return (
    <ul>
      {allPosts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

**In Client Components (with `use` API):**
```typescript
// app/blog/page.tsx (Server Component)
import Posts from '@/app/ui/posts'
import { Suspense } from 'react'

export default function Page() {
  // Don't await - pass promise to client
  const posts = getPosts()

  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
      <Posts posts={posts} />
    </Suspense>
  )
}

// app/ui/posts.tsx (Client Component)
'use client'
import { use } from 'react'

export default function Posts({
  posts,
}: {
  posts: Promise<{ id: string; title: string }[]>
}) {
  const allPosts = use(posts)

  return (
    <ul>
      {allPosts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

**Client Components with Community Libraries:**
```typescript
'use client'
import useSWR from 'swr'

const fetcher = (url) => fetch(url).then((r) => r.json())

export default function BlogPage() {
  const { data, error, isLoading } = useSWR(
    'https://api.vercel.app/blog',
    fetcher
  )

  if (isLoading) return <div>Loading...</div>
  if (error) return <div>Error: {error.message}</div>

  return (
    <ul>
      {data.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

**Caching & Request Deduplication:**
```typescript
// Fetch with cache control
export default async function Page() {
  // Cache for 1 hour
  const data = await fetch('https://...', {
    next: { revalidate: 3600 }
  })

  // Force cache
  const cached = await fetch('https://...', {
    cache: 'force-cache'
  })

  // Disable cache
  const dynamic = await fetch('https://...', {
    cache: 'no-store'
  })
}

// React.cache for request memoization
import { cache } from 'react'

export const getPost = cache(async (id: string) => {
  const post = await db.query.posts.findFirst({
    where: eq(posts.id, parseInt(id))
  })
  return post
})
```

---

### 7. generateStaticParams

**What It Does:**
- Replaces `getStaticPaths` from Pages Router
- Pre-generates dynamic routes at build time
- Returns array of `params` objects for dynamic segments

**Usage:**
```typescript
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx

export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://.../posts').then((res) => res.json())

  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }))
}

// This generates static pages for each slug at build time
export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const post = await getPost(slug)

  return <h1>{post.title}</h1>
}
```

**Multiple Dynamic Segments:**
```typescript
// app/docs/[lang]/[version]/page.tsx

export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const versions = await getVersions()

  return versions.flatMap((version) =>
    ['en', 'fr', 'es'].map((lang) => ({
      lang,
      version,
    }))
  )
}

export default function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ lang: string; version: string }>
}) {
  return <div>Documentation</div>
}
```

**Without generateStaticParams:**
- Route falls back to dynamic rendering at request time
- Can be slow for static routes
- Include `loading.tsx` to improve UX

---

### 8. LOADING STATES (Streaming and Suspense)

**With loading.tsx:**
```typescript
// app/blog/loading.tsx
export default function Loading() {
  return <div>Loading...</div>
}

// Behind scenes: Wraps page in <Suspense>, prefetches layout + loading UI
```

**With <Suspense>:**
```typescript
// app/blog/page.tsx
import { Suspense } from 'react'
import BlogList from '@/components/BlogList'
import BlogListSkeleton from '@/components/BlogListSkeleton'

export default function BlogPage() {
  return (
    <div>
      <header>
        <h1>Welcome to the Blog</h1>
      </header>
      <main>
        {/* Content outside boundary renders immediately */}
        {/* Content inside boundary shows fallback while loading */}
        <Suspense fallback={<BlogListSkeleton />}>
          <BlogList />
        </Suspense>
      </main>
    </div>
  )
}
```

**Sequential Data Fetching with Streaming:**
```typescript
// One request blocks the next
export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ username: string }>
}) {
  const { username } = await params
  const artist = await getArtist(username)

  return (
    <>
      <h1>{artist.name}</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading playlists...</div>}>
        <Playlists artistID={artist.id} />
      </Suspense>
    </>
  )
}

async function Playlists({ artistID }: { artistID: string }) {
  const playlists = await getArtistPlaylists(artistID)
  return <ul>{/* ... */}</ul>
}
```

**Parallel Data Fetching:**
```typescript
// Requests start simultaneously
export default async function Page({ params }) {
  const { username } = await params

  // Initiate both requests
  const artistData = getArtist(username)
  const albumsData = getAlbums(username)

  // Wait for both
  const [artist, albums] = await Promise.all([artistData, albumsData])

  return <div>{artist.name}</div>
}
```

**Benefits of Streaming:**
- Immediate navigation and visual feedback
- Shared layouts remain interactive
- Improved Core Web Vitals (TTFB, FCP, TTI)

---

### 9. CLIENT vs SERVER COMPONENTS

**Server Components (Default):**
- Run only on server
- **Can:**
  - Fetch data and access databases directly
  - Keep sensitive info (API keys, tokens) safe
  - Reduce JavaScript sent to browser
  - Reduce client-side rendering work
- **Cannot:**
  - Use hooks (useState, useEffect, etc.)
  - Use browser APIs (localStorage, window, etc.)
  - Use event listeners (`onClick`, `onChange`)

**Client Components (with `'use client'` directive):**
- Run on browser
- **Can:**
  - Use state, effects, and event listeners
  - Use browser-only APIs
  - Use custom hooks
- **Cannot:**
  - Directly fetch from databases
  - Access secrets/API keys

**Key Pattern - Composition:**
```typescript
// app/page.tsx (Server Component)
import LikeButton from '@/app/ui/like-button'

export default async function Page({ params }) {
  const post = await getPost(params.id)

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      {/* Pass server data as props to client component */}
      <LikeButton likes={post.likes} />
    </div>
  )
}

// app/ui/like-button.tsx (Client Component)
'use client'

import { useState } from 'react'

export default function LikeButton({ likes }: { likes: number }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(likes)

  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>
      {count} likes
    </button>
  )
}
```

**Reduce JavaScript Bundle:**
```typescript
// app/layout.tsx - Keep as Server Component
import Search from './search' // Client Component

export default function Layout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <>
      <nav>
        <Logo />  {/* Static, Server Component */}
        <Search /> {/* Interactive, Client Component */}
      </nav>
      <main>{children}</main>
    </>
  )
}

// app/search.tsx - Only this is Client Component
'use client'
export default function Search() {
  // Search logic
}
```

**Sharing Data Between Server & Client with Context:**
```typescript
// app/theme-provider.tsx (Client Component)
'use client'

import { createContext } from 'react'

export const ThemeContext = createContext({})

export default function ThemeProvider({ children }) {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
      {children}
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  )
}

// app/layout.tsx (Server Component imports Client Component)
import ThemeProvider from './theme-provider'

export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>
        <ThemeProvider>{children}</ThemeProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  )
}
```

**Sharing Data with React.cache:**
```typescript
// app/lib/user.ts
import { cache } from 'react'

export const getUser = cache(async () => {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/user')
  return res.json()
})

// Server Component calls it
// Client Component gets promise via context
// Same data, single request per render pass
```

**Preventing Environment Poisoning:**
```typescript
// app/lib/data.ts
import 'server-only' // Errors if imported in Client Component

export async function getData() {
  const res = await fetch('https://...', {
    headers: {
      authorization: process.env.API_KEY, // Secret!
    },
  })
  return res.json()
}
```

---

### 10. ROUTE STRUCTURE & FILE CONVENTIONS

**File System Routing:**
| Path | URL | Purpose |
|------|-----|---------|
| `app/layout.tsx` | — | Root layout (required, wraps all) |
| `app/page.tsx` | `/` | Home page |
| `app/blog/layout.tsx` | — | Blog layout (wraps blog routes) |
| `app/blog/page.tsx` | `/blog` | Blog list page |
| `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx` | `/blog/my-post` | Dynamic blog post |
| `app/blog/[slug]/loading.tsx` | — | Loading UI while post loads |
| `app/blog/[slug]/error.tsx` | — | Error boundary for post errors |
| `app/blog/[slug]/not-found.tsx` | — | 404 UI if post doesn't exist |
| `app/api/route.ts` | `/api` | API endpoint |
| `app/api/posts/route.ts` | `/api/posts` | API for posts |

**Dynamic Routes:**
```typescript
// [id] - Single dynamic segment
app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx → /blog/my-post

// [...slug] - Catch-all (greedy)
app/shop/[...category]/page.tsx → /shop, /shop/electronics, /shop/electronics/phones

// [[...slug]] - Optional catch-all
app/docs/[[...slug]]/page.tsx → /docs, /docs/api, /docs/api/fetch
```

**Route Groups (Organize without affecting URL):**
```typescript
// (marketing) and (shop) don't appear in URL
app/(marketing)/page.tsx → /
app/(marketing)/about/page.tsx → /about
app/(shop)/cart/page.tsx → /cart

// Can have separate layouts
app/(marketing)/layout.tsx → Only for marketing routes
app/(shop)/layout.tsx → Only for shop routes
```

**Private Folders (Not routable):**
```typescript
app/blog/_components/Post.tsx → Colocate components
app/blog/_lib/utils.ts → Colocate utilities
// These don't create routes, just help organize code
```

---

### 11. CACHING & REVALIDATION

**Fetch-based Caching:**
```typescript
// Default: Not cached
const data = await fetch('https://...')

// Cache for 1 hour (ISR - Incremental Static Regeneration)
const data = await fetch('https://...', {
  next: { revalidate: 3600 }
})

// Force cache
const data = await fetch('https://...', {
  cache: 'force-cache'
})

// Disable cache (always fresh)
const data = await fetch('https://...', {
  cache: 'no-store'
})

// Tag for on-demand revalidation
const data = await fetch('https://...', {
  next: { tags: ['blog'] }
})
```

**Revalidation Methods:**
```typescript
// app/lib/actions.ts (Server Action)
import { revalidateTag, revalidatePath } from 'next/cache'

export async function updatePost(formData: FormData) {
  // Update database

  // Option 1: Revalidate by tag
  revalidateTag('blog')

  // Option 2: Revalidate by path
  revalidatePath('/blog')
}
```

**Cache Components with `use cache`:**
```typescript
// app/blog/page.tsx
import { cacheLife } from 'next/cache'

export default async function Page() {
  'use cache'
  cacheLife('hours')  // Cache for 1 hour

  const posts = await db.query('SELECT * FROM posts')

  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

---

### 12. NAVIGATION & PREFETCHING

**Link Component (Automatic Prefetching):**
```typescript
import Link from 'next/link'

export default function Navigation() {
  return (
    <>
      {/* Prefetched when link enters viewport or hovered */}
      <Link href="/blog">Blog</Link>

      {/* No prefetching */}
      <Link href="/blog" prefetch={false}>Blog</Link>

      {/* Prefetch only on hover */}
      <Link href="/blog" prefetch="on-hover">Blog</Link>
    </>
  )
}
```

**Router Navigation:**
```typescript
'use client'

import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation'

export function Component() {
  const router = useRouter()

  return (
    <button onClick={() => router.push('/dashboard')}>
      Go to Dashboard
    </button>
  )
}
```

**Static vs Dynamic Prefetching:**
- **Static routes:** Full route prefetched
- **Dynamic routes:** Partially prefetched (layout + loading.tsx only)

---

## RECOMMENDED READING FILES

The files I thoroughly read for this summary:

